1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:06,150
I was about 13 when an uncle gave me a copy of Jostein Gaarder’s Sophie’s World.
我13岁左右的时候，一位叔叔送给我一本乔斯坦·贾德的《苏菲的世界》。

2
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:12,140
It was full of ideas that were new to me, so I spent the summer with my head in and out of that book.
书中满是我从未接触过的思想，所以整个夏天我都时而埋头读这本书，时而又从书中抬起头来。

3
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:15,960
It spoke to me and brought me into a world of philosophy.
它打动了我，把我带进了哲学的世界。

4
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:20,070
That love for philosophy lasted until I got to college.
这份对哲学的热爱一直持续到我上大学。

5
00:00:20,870 --> 00:00:29,870
Nothing kills the love for philosophy faster than people who think they understand Foucault, Baudrillard, or Confucius better than you — and then try to explain them.
最能迅速扼杀对哲学的热爱的，莫过于那些自以为比你更懂福柯、鲍德里亚或孔子的人——接着还要试图给你讲解这些哲学家的思想。

6
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:38,042
Eric Weiner’s The Socrates Express: In Search of Life Lessons from Dead Philosophers reawakened my love for philosophy.
埃里克·韦纳的《苏格拉底快车：向已故哲学家寻求人生启示》重新唤起了我对哲学的热爱。

7
00:00:38,842 --> 00:00:43,852
It is not an explanation but an invitation to think about and experience philosophy.
这本书不是讲解，而是邀请——邀请人们去思考、去体验哲学。

8
00:00:44,652 --> 00:00:54,002
Weiner starts each chapter with a scene on a train ride between cities and then frames each philosopher’s work in the context of one thing they can help us do better.
韦纳在每一章的开头都描写一段城际火车旅途中的场景，然后围绕每位哲学家能帮助我们做得更好的某一件事，来阐述这位哲学家的思想著述。

9
00:00:54,802 --> 00:01:03,782
The end result is a read in which we learn to wonder like Socrates, see like Thoreau, listen like Schopenhauer, and have no regrets like Nietzsche.
最终的成果是这样一本读物：我们从中学会像苏格拉底那样好奇，像梭罗那样观察，像叔本华那样倾听，像尼采那样不留遗憾。

10
00:01:04,582 --> 00:01:11,112
This, more than a book about understanding philosophy, is a book about learning to use philosophy to improve a life.
与其说这是一本关于理解哲学的书，不如说它是一本关于学会运用哲学来改善生活的书。

11
00:01:11,912 --> 00:01:19,782
He makes philosophical thought an appealing exercise that improves the quality of our experiences, and he does so with plenty of humor.
他把哲学思考变成一项引人入胜的思维活动，能提升我们种种体验的品质，而且他的笔调充满幽默。

12
00:01:20,582 --> 00:01:31,902
Weiner enters into conversation with some of the most important philosophers in history, and he becomes part of that crowd in the process by decoding their messages and adding his own interpretation.
韦纳与历史上一些最重要的哲学家展开对话，并在此过程中解读他们传达的思想、加入自己的阐释，从而成为他们当中的一员。

13
00:01:32,702 --> 00:01:42,972
The Socrates Express is a fun, sharp book that draws readers in with its apparent simplicity and gradually pulls them into deeper thoughts on desire, loneliness, and aging.
《苏格拉底快车》是一本有趣而犀利的书，它以看似简单的面貌吸引读者，再逐渐把他们带入对欲望、孤独和衰老更深入的思考。

14
00:01:43,772 --> 00:01:49,264
The invitation is clear: Weiner wants you to pick up a coffee or tea and sit down with this book.
这份邀请很明确：韦纳希望你端起一杯咖啡或茶，坐下来读这本书。

15
00:01:50,064 --> 00:01:51,874
I encourage you to take his offer.
我鼓励你接受他的邀请。

16
00:01:52,674 --> 00:01:56,474
It’s worth your time, even if time is something we don’t have a lot of.
这本书值得你花时间去读，即使我们的时间并不充裕。
